'I will put on, I will wear fame; for long life may there be attainment of old age;

May we live a hundred full autumns; I will clothe myself for the increase of wealth;

May Heaven and Earth [clothe] me with fame, may Indra and Bṛhaspati with fame;

May Bhaga and fame not perish for me; may fame be fastened upon me.'

With these [verses] he puts on an unwashed garment.

While she looks, he mutters:

‘May [Prajāpati] place your heart in my vow; may your thought follow my thought;

listen to my word with a single mind; may Prajāpati unite you with me.’

"Very auspicious is this bride; assemble and look upon her;

having bestowed good fortune upon her, go disperse to your homes."

With this [formula] he blesses the onlookers as they depart.

He ties [her hair] together with living wool:

"Tying together the hair, straight and gentle, be a crest for all your friends;

be auspicious, being conveyed to a good family; be auspicious among the people together with the conveyances."

"Well-disposed I enter the houses, most gracious to heroic men;

bringing refreshment, sprinkling clarified butter, in them may I dwell together, well-disposed!" —

with this [verse] he enters the dwelling where the fire is kindled, which has water and plants, under [the asterism] Rohiṇī or Mūla, or whatever [asterism] is declared auspicious.

Then he places a student of the Veda in her lap [with the verse]:

"By Soma the Ādityas are strong; by Soma the earth is great;

yonder in the lap of these asterisms Soma is placed."

Having loosed her yoke-strap, he should bring the two together [with the verses]:

"I saw you in thought shining, exerting yourself in your own body in your season;

come up to me, high young woman, be fruitful with offspring, O you who desire a son!

Prajāpati, take pleasure in my body; Tvaṣṭṛ, the overpowering Indra with the gods;

in agreement with the All-gods, with the seasons, may we two become the parents of many males!

I placed the embryo in the plants; I, within all beings;

I generated creatures on the earth; I, sons in the subsequent wives!" —

“Let King Soma separate [them], Agni separating both;

let Agni the conveyer of oblations remain right here; drive away the corpse-devouring one!”

With these words, having placed the fire on a mat or on an [earthen] vessel, he sends it off.

On the full moon of Āgrahāyaṇa, having cooked a pot-cooked offering in milk, he offers of it:

'White-footed, it seized the water before and after;
and these seven subjects of Varuṇa, all related to the king, svāhā!

The white horse establishes the furious ones, the bull bearing the embryo quickly for a long time;
Men putting on a year-long wheel-covering; you are revealed, drive out this entire [evil]!

To the white one, the son of Ruśigu, svāhā! Truly, in the presence of the white one, no snake has slain anything;
To the white one, the descendant of Vītahavya, svāhā!

May there be safety for us from the descendants of Prajāpati, svāhā!'—

'Arise, life, breath has come to us; darkness has departed, light approaches;
It has cleared the path for the sun to go; we have come to where they prolong our life!'—
with this, they arise, the youngest first.

If he should see a bad dream, having offered sesame seeds with the vyāhṛtis, he should worship the directions:

"May Bodha and Pratibodha protect me from the east; / and may Asvapna and Anavadrāṇa protect me from the south;

May Gopāyamāna and Rakṣamāṇa protect me from the west; / and may Jāgṛvi and Arundhatī protect me from the north;

May Viṣṇu, the earth, and the Nāgas protect me from below; / and may the Bṛhaspatis, the Viśvedevas, and the sky protect me from above."
